public class OrderProcessor {
    public List<OrderItem> processOrders(List<RawOrderItem> rawData) {
        return rawData.stream()
            .map(this::transformItem)
            .collect(Collectors.toList());
    }

    private OrderItem transformItem(RawOrderItem raw) {
        // Business logic
        double finalPrice = raw.getPrice();
        if (raw.getIsDiscounted() != null && raw.getIsDiscounted()) {
            finalPrice = raw.getPrice() * 0.9; // 10% discount
        }

        return new OrderItem(
            raw.getProductId(),
            raw.getQuantity(),
            raw.getPrice(),
            finalPrice,
            raw.getIsDiscounted() != null ? raw.getIsDiscounted() : false
        );
    }
}
```

This refactoring prevents ClassCastExceptions because parameterized collections ensure type consistency at compile time rather than runtime. Heap pollution is eliminated since the JVM can now guarantee that objects retrieved from collections are of the expected type. Additionally, POJOs centralize validation logic in constructors, preventing malformed data from propagating through the system - invalid OrderItems simply cannot be created, making debugging easier and reducing error-prone manual validation scattered throughout processing code.

