r takes an item, it calls `notifyAll()` to wake up a sleeping Producer thread, letting it know there is now space in the buffer.

### 💡 Modern Practical Alternative
While the bare-metal `wait()` and `notifyAll()` code is great for learning computer science fundamentals, **you should rarely write this in production code.**

Modern languages provide built-in concurrency collections. In Java, you would simply use a `BlockingQueue`. It handles all the locking, waiting, and waking up for you automatically:

```java
import java.util.concurrent.ArrayBlockingQueue;
import java.util.concurrent.BlockingQueue;

public class ModernProducerConsumer {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Thread-safe bounded buffer that handles all synchronization internally
        BlockingQueue<Integer> buffer = new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(5);

        // Producer
        new Thread(() -> {
            try {
                for(int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
                    buffer.put(i); // Automatically waits if full
                    System.out.println("Produced: " + i);
                }
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {}
        }).start();

        // Consumer
        new Thread(() -> {
            try {
                while(true) {
                    Integer item = buffer.take(); // Automatically waits if empty
                    System.out.println("Consumed: " + item);
                }
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {}
        }).start();
    }
}
