    </div>
  );
};

export default UserProfileCard;
```

This approach prevents three specific bug classes: 1) Missing required fields like `name` or `email` that would cause undefined access errors, 2) Invalid prop combinations such as non-existent display modes or themes that break styling logic, and 3) Malformed data like invalid URLs or non-email strings that would create broken images or incorrect text display.

The validation happens at the component boundary, catching errors before they propagate into your rendering logic. This makes debugging much easier since you get clear error messages about exactly which props are invalid rather than cryptic runtime exceptions deep in your JSX.

